If it is not an array, hence the exclamation

273
00:11:21,294 --> 00:11:24,002
mark, then I wanna turn it in one so that

274
00:11:24,002 --> 00:11:26,278
the spread operator always works because it

275
00:11:26,278 --> 00:11:28,263
needs an array. So I'll turn the first

276
00:11:28,263 --> 00:11:30,265
element into an array if it's not one

277
00:11:30,361 --> 00:11:33,390
already. We can do this by overriding setAL

278
00:11:35,189 --> 00:11:37,583
with a new array which simply includes

279
00:11:37,776 --> 00:11:38,065
setAL.

280
00:11:38,676 --> 00:11:41,006
So if it wasn't an array yet, it will now be

281
00:11:41,135 --> 00:11:43,191
one. And of course, in order to be able to

282
00:11:43,287 --> 00:11:44,892
override this, we should turn this from a

283
00:11:44,892 --> 00:11:47,844
const to a let, so to a regular variable.

284
00:11:48,984 --> 00:11:49,946
And now with that,

285
00:11:50,588 --> 00:11:52,562
if we try this again, we still have 16

286
00:11:52,658 --> 00:11:55,066
elements. But now if we expand this, we see

287
00:11:55,082 --> 00:11:57,585
we have no nested arrays, but we have a flat

288
00:11:57,665 --> 00:12:00,217
structure where we have blue S round neck,

289
00:12:00,281 --> 00:12:03,266
blue S V-neck, blue M round neck, and so on.

290
00:12:03,346 --> 00:12:05,561
So where we have all those combinations.

291
00:12:06,315 --> 00:12:09,188
And this approach would not just work for 3

292
00:12:09,188 --> 00:12:11,340
sets now but for any amount of sets.

293
00:12:11,934 --> 00:12:14,022
So this would be a more generic version of

294
00:12:14,022 --> 00:12:16,673
this Cartesian product algorithm which works

295
00:12:16,673 --> 00:12:19,162
with multiple sets. And the only thing we

296
00:12:19,243 --> 00:12:21,347
did here is we took advantage of this

297
00:12:21,347 --> 00:12:22,134
existing array,

298
00:12:22,841 --> 00:12:25,587
fine-tuned it slightly to work with nested

299
00:12:25,587 --> 00:12:26,117
arrays too,

300
00:12:26,792 --> 00:12:28,992
and then made sure that we go through all

301
00:12:29,201 --> 00:12:31,643
the input elements we might get in a for

302
00:12:31,755 --> 00:12:35,197
loop and call our original function for only

303
00:12:35,230 --> 00:12:36,895
2 sets at a time.

304
00:12:37,655 --> 00:12:39,596
And this adjustment to work with nested

305
00:12:39,596 --> 00:12:41,520
arrays and to flatten them was needed

306
00:12:41,924 --> 00:12:43,930
because we do create that temporary

307
00:12:43,962 --> 00:12:46,048
Cartesian product here, which will be an

308
00:12:46,048 --> 00:12:47,003
array full of arrays,

309
00:12:47,601 --> 00:12:49,834
and that's one level of nesting too much,

310
00:12:50,092 --> 00:12:52,502
which is why we're using the spread operator

311
00:12:52,535 --> 00:12:54,703
here in our main cartProduct function.

312
00:12:56,290 --> 00:12:57,856
Now for the time complexity.

313
00:12:58,606 --> 00:13:00,775
Previously I said that we had big O of n

314
00:13:00,808 --> 00:13:03,536
times m, but that we could generalize this

315
00:13:03,648 --> 00:13:06,474
to quadratic time if we say we only wanna

316
00:13:06,602 --> 00:13:08,866
look at the length of one of the 2 input

317
00:13:08,866 --> 00:13:09,236
sets.

318
00:13:10,231 --> 00:13:13,250
Now I mentioned this possible generalization

319
00:13:13,523 --> 00:13:16,397
because we need that generalization here if

320
00:13:16,413 --> 00:13:18,307
we have an unlimited amount of sets.

321
00:13:18,901 --> 00:13:21,197
Because now theoretically, of course,

322
00:13:21,872 --> 00:13:24,360
the time complexity and the space complexity

323
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:24,649
as well

324
00:13:25,565 --> 00:13:27,299
would be n times m

325
00:13:27,957 --> 00:13:30,576
times Q times, times L times whatever you

326
00:13:30,624 --> 00:13:33,725
want for as many input sets as you have.

327
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:36,649
But this is not how you want to express this

328
00:13:36,649 --> 00:13:39,188
because you don't know with how many input

329
00:13:39,284 --> 00:13:41,518
values your algorithm is going to be called.

330
00:13:42,096 --> 00:13:42,626
For 2

331
00:13:43,398 --> 00:13:45,679
input values as we had it before, it was

332
00:13:45,727 --> 00:13:48,571
clear that you had, well, 2 values. But now

333
00:13:48,619 --> 00:13:51,351
you can't assume how many sets will be

334
00:13:51,351 --> 00:13:53,728
passed in. So we can't express it like this

335
00:13:53,728 --> 00:13:56,661
as a product of all these set lengths. We

336
00:13:56,661 --> 00:13:58,648
need a more general way of expressing it

337
00:13:58,648 --> 00:14:00,715
instead. That's why we have the big O

338
00:14:00,715 --> 00:14:01,901
notation in the first place.

339
00:14:02,990 --> 00:14:05,378
So it would be nice if we could express this

340
00:14:05,378 --> 00:14:06,371
in a more general way,

341
00:14:06,900 --> 00:14:09,271
and therefore we'll take this generalization

342
00:14:09,383 --> 00:14:10,809
I mentioned in the last lecture.

343
00:14:11,546 --> 00:14:14,687
We assume that the length of the longest

344
00:14:14,687 --> 00:14:17,122
array is the length of all the input arrays.

345
00:14:17,475 --> 00:14:19,638
That might not be the case, but this is the

346
00:14:19,718 --> 00:14:21,977
worst-case approximation we can make here.

347
00:14:22,459 --> 00:14:24,661
So let's say this length is

348
00:14:25,239 --> 00:14:25,384
n.

349
00:14:27,023 --> 00:14:28,759
So we would have n, but of course that's not

350
00:14:28,856 --> 00:14:31,010
all. The length of the arrays is not all we

351
00:14:31,010 --> 00:14:31,172
have.

352
00:14:32,016 --> 00:14:34,758
Instead, it also matters how many arrays we

353
00:14:34,758 --> 00:14:37,614
have. Because before, with the 2 sets where

354
00:14:37,614 --> 00:14:39,690
we knew that it always were 2 sets, we had n

355
00:14:39,802 --> 00:14:40,413
times m

356
00:14:41,152 --> 00:14:41,747
as the

357
00:14:42,277 --> 00:14:44,768
Big O notation, or the more generalized form

358
00:14:45,250 --> 00:14:45,909
O.

359
00:14:47,114 --> 00:14:49,961
Now we had squared here as this worst-case

360
00:14:49,961 --> 00:14:50,507
generalization.

361
00:14:51,512 --> 00:14:54,125
Because we knew that we had 2 sets because

362
00:14:54,157 --> 00:14:55,728
it was in the end n times n,

363
00:14:56,305 --> 00:14:58,582
right? The length of the first set times the

364
00:14:58,582 --> 00:14:59,736
length of the second set.

365
00:15:01,050 --> 00:15:03,519
And if we had 3 sets and we knew that it

366
00:15:03,599 --> 00:15:06,084
always were 3 sets which we got, we would

367
00:15:06,084 --> 00:15:08,489
have n times n times n. And that of course

368
00:15:08,569 --> 00:15:11,294
is just cubic notation, cubic time

369
00:15:11,294 --> 00:15:13,234
complexity, so n at the power of 3.

370
00:15:14,196 --> 00:15:16,360
And therefore, if we don't know the number

371
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:19,314
of arrays in advance, we of course can know

372
00:15:19,957 --> 00:15:20,792
that it should be

373
00:15:21,322 --> 00:15:23,363
n at the power of x when it comes to the

374
00:15:23,363 --> 00:15:26,367
time complexity, where x is the number of

375
00:15:26,399 --> 00:15:27,090
input arrays.

376
00:15:27,765 --> 00:15:28,680
Because before,

377
00:15:29,275 --> 00:15:33,259
the power was the number of arrays. We had O

378
00:15:33,628 --> 00:15:37,243
for 2 sets and n at the power of 3 for 3

379
00:15:37,243 --> 00:15:37,677
sets.

380
00:15:38,207 --> 00:15:39,942
So the more general form is n at the power

381
00:15:39,990 --> 00:15:42,673
of x, where x is the amount of input sets

382
00:15:42,705 --> 00:15:45,388
we're getting, in this case 3. And n is then

383
00:15:45,517 --> 00:15:48,891
our worst-case length, so the length of the

384
00:15:48,891 --> 00:15:49,855
longest set.

385
00:15:50,819 --> 00:15:52,265
And that's the same here for the space

386
00:15:52,265 --> 00:15:52,907
complexity.

387
00:15:53,952 --> 00:15:55,783
So what this essentially means for the time

388
00:15:55,783 --> 00:15:56,892
complexity especially

389
00:15:57,551 --> 00:16:01,053
is that if we now have 3 arrays as an input,

390
00:16:01,744 --> 00:16:04,797
we would have 3 as a value for x, and we

391
00:16:04,797 --> 00:16:06,580
would then take the length of the longest

392
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:09,119
array, in this case this one here, 4, and

393
00:16:09,135 --> 00:16:10,404
that would be our value for n.

394
00:16:10,982 --> 00:16:12,909
So we would have 4 to the power of 3

395
00:16:13,776 --> 00:16:16,393
execution steps, which we would assume. Now

396
00:16:16,441 --> 00:16:19,058
in reality, we will probably have less steps

397
00:16:19,170 --> 00:16:21,578
because not all arrays have a length of 4,

398
00:16:21,899 --> 00:16:23,665
but again, that's our worst-case

399
00:16:23,665 --> 00:16:24,596
generalization.

400
00:16:25,351 --> 00:16:27,261
And therefore, the Cartesian product has a

401
00:16:27,261 --> 00:16:29,766
general time complexity of n to the power of

402
00:16:29,830 --> 00:16:32,864
x for an unlimited amount of input arrays

403
00:16:33,282 --> 00:16:34,983
with any length of your choice.
